<div class="article-meta"><span>Selected Work</span><span>1993–1996</span><span>TICSA / Internet Africa</span></div>
<h1>Building the commercial Internet</h1>
<p class="lede">In 1993, TICSA began operating with a single 64 Kbps international connection and four original customers. I joined as the first full-time employee and network engineer, but the role quickly became much broader.</p>
<div class="article-content">
<h2>Starting with the network</h2>
<p>The early network was built around a Cisco 4000 router and Frame Relay connections terminating on PCs running KA9Q as IP packet routers. A Sun Sparc Classic server provided services including NNTP news feeds, UUCP email, SMTP and other applications. One of my early projects was installing Gopher, before the Web became the dominant interface to the Internet.</p>
<h2>From infrastructure to an ISP operation</h2>
<p>As the business grew, the technology changed rapidly: standalone dial-up modems gave way to modem banks and ISDN PRI circuits; corporate leased lines evolved through multiplexed and channelised circuits; and the server network became a dedicated operation. I remained on the networking side, helping grow the network and build a Network Operations Centre.</p>
<h2>The first commercial growth</h2>
<p>By 1996, dial-up had gained significant momentum and a corporate customer base was developing. The national and international network had expanded substantially, with major investment in infrastructure and staffing. The experience became the foundation for the next stage of the career: moving from hands-on engineering into product, commercial and executive technology leadership.</p>
<h2>What this chapter demonstrates</h2>
<p>The lasting lesson is the breadth of an early-stage technology role. Building an Internet service required network engineering, systems, operations, customer support, infrastructure planning and practical business judgement — often performed by the same small team.</p>
</div>
</article>
